{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>See, I am giving, etc.</b> One needs to know why He used the language of seeing for these matters. Further, His saying <b>I</b>. Further, why did He use singular language, since the entire parashah as a whole was spoken in plural language." "And it appears that the intention is this: since He came to choose for them the supernal world and to diminish all the goods of this world, and in order that the matter should be verified for them, the one who reproves must possess two qualities: first, that he have a tangible recognition of the supernal good; and second, that he have attained attainment of the good of this world and its possessions. For if the reproofer were devoid of the possessions of this world, his words would not be believed by those who grasp the benefits of this world, for they would say: one does not say to one who has not seen, come and tell; if he had tasted the tastes of the great pleasures of this world and its possessions and its delights, he would not despise them and choose their inferior — and in this they would not incline their ear to accept his words. Likewise, one who comes to magnify the good of the World to Come — his words would not be justified nor believed by the natural intellect at all if he himself speaks only from faith and has neither seen nor known. Therefore the savior and great man, our master Moses, said <b>See, I</b> — meaning: look to me, and you will justify and gain understanding to vindicate the matters, for he attained recognition of the good of this world, since he was exceedingly great and mighty, a king and enormously wealthy, as they said of blessed memory (Shabbat 92a), and ״my beloved is radiant and pleasant״, and the supernal good too he recognized, for he ascended to heaven and tasted a supernal taste. And after he attained tangible recognition — the recognition of two things and their attainment — he said to them the things spoken regarding the matter, as I shall explain. And he said ״See״ in singular, because for this seeing they are all equal in its recognition as one person, since Moses' attainment in this world and the World to Come is manifest to all." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend in the statement <b>See, I</b> along the lines of what they said of blessed memory (Rambam, Hilkhot Teshuvah, ch. 5): a person is able to make himself like Moses our master — and this is what he alerted them, that they should look to him to emulate him, everyone who comes to draw near to the service of holiness, the service of HaShem, and not compare themselves with what is beneath them and regard themselves as having found much. And he spoke in singular language — perhaps because he intended this allusion only for the individuals of the nation who prepare themselves for supernal attainment, for who and who is worthy to attain this attainment to be like Moses? And corresponding to the rest of the generality of Israel he said ״I set before you.״" "<b>And by way of</b> allusion, he hints according to their words of blessed memory who said (Zohar, vol. 3, 253a) that all children of Torah have a spark from Moses, and needless to say that generation who heard from his mouth that they are a part of him. And this is what he alluded to in this statement <b>See, I</b> — meaning, as though speaking with himself and saying to himself ״I,״ for all Israel trace their lineage back to him, and when he speaks with them it is as though he speaks with himself. And therefore he also said ״See״ in singular language corresponding to the measure of ״I,״ which is singular language — one says ״See״ about it." "Further, he alludes to HaShem who called and said ״I.״ And this is the parsing of the verse: ״See״ — for that One who called and said to you ״I,״ He it is who is ״giving before you,״ etc. And His saying <b>See</b> is singular language, for on the scale of this matter they will all be equal as one to know that HaShem is the One who gives them blessing, etc." "<b>I set before you this day, etc.</b> Meaning: because this day they attained full understanding, as they said of blessed memory (Avodah Zarah 5b): a person does not attain his master's understanding until forty years." "<b>Further,</b> it may be explained by noting further the verse's saying <b>a blessing and a curse</b> — for it should have needed to say only ״et the blessing, etc. and the curse,״ etc., as it says afterward. However, it may be explained according to the way of the Sages of blessed memory (Sifrei), who said: Moses said to them: you see the wicked prospering for two or three days, but their end is to be rejected — end of quotation. And this is the verse's saying <b>this day a blessing and a curse</b> — meaning, a blessing that is also a curse, namely the tranquility of the wicked in this world, which lasts only a brief time, since this world is called ״one day,״ in the manner of the verse (Deuteronomy 7:11) ״הַיּוֹם לַעֲשׂוֹתָם״ (Eiruvin 22a). And this is what it was precise in saying ״this day״ adjacent to the mention of blessing — to say that it is not a blessing except for this day and not for tomorrow, and indeed it is a curse. It follows that the good of this world is slight and drags a curse in its wake." "<b>Further,</b> it may be explained according to their statement of blessed memory (Sifrei), who said: a parable of one who sat at a crossroads, and before him were two paths: one whose beginning was level ground and whose end was thorns, and one whose beginning was thorns and whose end was level ground, and he would inform the passersby and say to them: ״you see this path whose beginning is level ground for two or three steps, etc., and its end,״ etc. — end of quotation. And this is the verse's saying <b>I set before you</b>, etc. — meaning two kinds of goods, and in each there is blessing and curse, for the way of the wicked has level ground at the beginning and thorns at the end — behold, blessing and curse; and the way of life also has thorns at its beginning and level ground at its end — behold, blessing and curse as well. And it returned and specified <b>et the blessing</b> — meaning, to this shall be called blessing, for one should not be sensitive to the branch of curse within it — <b>which you shall heed</b>, etc. — even though there is suffering in this midst, for the righteous suffer in this world in the struggle of the intellect, and their world is hard for them in what they must endure in it; <b>and the curse if you do not heed</b> — even though you indulge in the world with every desire of the animal soul and it is pleasant to you, this level ground shall be reckoned as a curse." "<b>Further,</b> the entire verse may be explained along this path: because He said to them just before (Deuteronomy 11:24) regarding the inheritance of the land, ״כָּל הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר תִּדְרֹךְ כַּף רַגְלְכֶם בּוֹ לָכֶם יִהְיֶה,״ therefore He said to them <b>Behold, I set</b>, etc. — meaning, this gift that HaShem gives you this day. It was precise in saying <b>this day</b>, for this blessing that He told them, ״every place,״ etc., was not said to them until this day. And His saying <b>a blessing and a curse</b> means: in this gift there is blessing and there is curse. And it returned and specified <b>et the blessing which you shall heed</b> — meaning, if you heed, this gift shall be for you a blessing; and if you do not heed, it is nothing for you but a curse, for in its midst the nations will envy you and destroy you from it with great vengeance. Also, the enjoyment of it will be to them for harm in the eternal world, in the manner of the verse (Deuteronomy 7:10) ״וּמְשַׁלֵּם לְשׂוֹנְאָיו אֶל פָּנָיו לְהַאֲבִידוֹ.״ And see what follows:"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:17:07.296126101Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}